kaga shogunate. Ieyasu became the shōgun, and the Tokugawa clan governed Japan from Edo Castle in the eastern city of Edo (Tokyo) along with the daimyō lords of the samurai class.\nThe Tokugawa shogunate organized Japanese society under the strict Tokugawa class system and banned the entry of most foreigners under the isolationist policies of sakoku to promote political stability."}],"facts":[{"qid":"Q205662","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q171977","value_label":"Tokugawa Ieyasu","from_t":1603,"to_t":1605,"title":"head of state","property":"P35","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Tokugawa Ieyasu"},{"qid":"Q205662","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q314464","value_label":"Tokugawa Hidetada","from_t":1605,"to_t":1623,"title":"head of state","property":"P35","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Tokugawa Hidetada"},{"qid":"Q205662","kind":"capital","value_qid":"Q215646","value_label":"Edo","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P36","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Edo"},{"qid":"Q205662","kind":"official_language","value_qid":"Q5326692","value_label":"Early Modern Japanese","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P37","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Early Modern Japanese"},{"qid":"Q205662","kind":"religion","value_qid":"Q748","value_label":"Buddhism","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P140","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Buddhism"},{"qid":"Q205662","kind":"religion","value_qid":"Q812767","value_label":"Shinto","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P140","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Shinto"},{"qid":"Q205662","kind":"government","value_qid":"Q849242","value_label":"hereditary monarchy","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P122","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"hereditary monarchy"},{"qid":"Q205662","kind":"predecessor","value_qid":"Q11634225","value_label":"Toyotomi government","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P1365","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Toyotomi government"},{"qid":"Q205662","kind":"successor","value_qid":"Q1322181","value_label":"Government of Meiji Japan","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P1366","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Government of Meiji Japan"},{"qid":"Q205662","kind":"successor","value_qid":"Q188712","value_label":"Empire of Japan","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P1366","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Empire of Japan"},{"qid":"Q205662","kind":"coat","value_qid":null,"value_label":"Tokugawa family crest.svg","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P94","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":null}],"people":{"Q171977":{"label":"Tokugawa Ieyasu","description":"founder and first shogun of the Tokugawa shogunate of Japan (1543–1616)","enwiki":"Tokugawa Ieyasu","summary":"Tokugawa Ieyasu (born Matsudaira Takechiyo; January 31, 1543 – June 1, 1616) was a Japanese samurai, daimyo and the founder and first shogun of the Tokugawa shogunate of Japan, which ruled from 1603 until the Meiji Restoration in 1868. He was the third of the three \"Great Unifiers\" of Japan, along with his former lord Oda Nobunaga and fellow Oda subordinate Toyotomi Hideyoshi. The son of a minor daimyo, Ieyasu once lived as a hostage under daimyo Imagawa Yoshimoto on behalf of his father. He later succeeded as daimyo after his father's death, serving as ally, vassal, and general of the Oda clan, and building up his strength under Oda Nobunaga.","image":"Tokugawa Ieyasu2.JPG","birth":1543.0821917808219,"death":1616.4153005464482},"Q314464":{"label":"Tokugawa Hidetada","description":"The second shogun of the Tokugawa shogunate of Japan (1579–1632)","enwiki":"Tokugawa Hidetada","summary":"Tokugawa Hidetada (徳川 秀忠; 2 May 1579 – 14 March 1632) was the second shōgun of the Tokugawa dynasty, who ruled from 1605 until his abdication in 1623. He was the third son of Tokugawa Ieyasu. Through his daughter Tokugawa Masako, he was also the maternal grandfather of Empress Meishō.","image":"Hidetada2.jpg","birth":1579.331506849315,"death":1632.1994535519125}},"med